In article <3A993403.3584EFEA@toyvax.Tucson.AZ.US>, Vance Haemmerle <vance@toyvax.Tucson.AZ.US> writes:
:I've been experimenting with dual-booting VMS and Tru64 on a DEC 3000
:system and have to reset the time each time I switch operating systems.
:Tru64 says that the TOY clock has a preposterous value while VMS just
:asks me to enter the time again at the initial phase of bootup.  I'm guessing
:that these operating systems use the hardware TOY clock in their own way....
:but why?

  Simply because the original groups of operating systems did not coordinate 
  the time formats used in the BB_WATCH -- this was left as an OS-specific
  (unarchitected) feature.  This disparity is obvious a problem (only) when 
  switching between operating systems, something that is and was not believed 
  to be a regular occurance and not something that would warrant one (or more)
  of the operating systems changing the BB_WATCH format used to a common 
  BB_WATCH time format...

